\fs96 \cf2 Self-referential structs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Some data structures don't fit well with value semantics. Lists, trees, and graphs all need nodes that point to each other. You can't build these by nesting one value inside another, because the type would keep growing forever.\
In Mojo, you build these shapes with pointers, heap allocation, and manual cleanup. The idea may feel new at first, but the pattern stays simple once you see it in small steps.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Avoid direct self-reference\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo won't let you build a type that stores another instance of itself, even when nested within an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Node\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  \cf15 next\cf5 :\cf14  Optional\cf5 [\cf14 Node\cf5 ]\cf14   \cf6 # ERROR: Recursive reference\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Each 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 struct
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has a fixed layout. If 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Node
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  held another 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Node
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  directly, the compiler wouldn't know how much space to reserve. Optional fields don't help, because the outer value still needs room for the inner one.\
Pointers solve this problem. Pointers have a fixed size, and they let values point at each other without blowing up the type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Adding self-referential pointers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The following code shows how to set up a node that can point to its own type. This sample gives you a node type with a value slot and a single link to the next node:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Node\cf5 [\cf14 ElementType\cf5 :\cf14  ImplicitlyCopyable \cf5 &\cf14  Writable\cf5 ](\cf14 Movable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  NodePointer \cf5 =\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 ,\cf14  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Optional\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf6 # The `Node`'s value\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  \cf15 next\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 NodePointer \cf6 # Pointer to the next `Node`\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Uses an `Optional` value to allow 'empty' Node construction\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # that can be moved into newly allocated memory\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Optional\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 None\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 value \cf5 =\cf14  value\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf15 next\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 NodePointer\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The code defines a type-specific 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 NodePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type alias built on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 UnsafePointer}}. Add the comptime within the struct definition, as shown here. Including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 MutExternalOrigin}} lets the pointer represent dynamically-allocated memory that won't be tracked by the lifetime checker. You'll need to both allocate and deallocate memory as needed.\
The optional 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  lets you create "empty" nodes, enabling you to move new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Node
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  memory allocations into place.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Building nodes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Here's the key pattern you'll use in many reference structures:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Allocate space.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Construct a value-holding node.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Move it into the allocated memory.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Return the pointer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 And here's an example of that pattern:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @staticmethod\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 make_node\cf5 (\cf14 value\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 NodePointer\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     node_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 ](\cf18 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     node_ptr\cf5 .\cf14 init_pointee_move\cf5 (\cf14 Self\cf5 (\cf14 value\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  node_ptr\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This "allocate space, initialize, and move" approach creates safe pointer-based structures in Mojo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Linking nodes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To link nodes, create a new node and set your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 next
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  pointer to it. This example shows how to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 append
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  a new node using a supplied value. If a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 next
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  node already exists, the code frees it before appending the new node.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 append\cf5 (mut\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # Free chain if replacing `next`\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 if\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf15 next\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf15 next\cf5 [].\cf14 free_chain\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf15 next\cf5 .\cf14 destroy_pointee\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf15 next\cf5 .\cf14 free\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf15 next\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 make_node\cf5 (\cf14 value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Walking the list\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To walk the list, follow the chain until you reach the end. Recursive code makes this easy to read. This example prints the value stored at each node:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @staticmethod\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 print_list\cf5 (\cf14 node_ptr\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 NodePointer\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 if\cf14  \cf5 not\cf14  node_ptr\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 return\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     current_value\cf5 :\cf14  Optional\cf5 [\cf14 Self\cf5 .\cf14 ElementType\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  node_ptr\cf5 [].\cf14 value\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 if\cf14  current_value\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 current_value\cf5 .\cf14 value\cf5 (),\cf14  end\cf5 =\cf19 " "\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 if\cf14  node_ptr\cf5 [].\cf15 next\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         Self\cf5 .\cf14 print_list\cf5 (\cf14 node_ptr\cf5 [].\cf15 next\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 else:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The pattern is simple: check the value, print it if it exists, then move to the next link.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 This example uses a static method, but you can also implement it as an instance method.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Cleaning up\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Because you allocate each node yourself, you're also responsible for freeing it. This cleanup walks the chain and frees each node after destroying its pointee:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 free_chain\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     current \cf5 =\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf15 next\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 while\cf14  current\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         next_node \cf5 =\cf14  current\cf5 [].\cf15 next\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         current\cf5 .\cf14 destroy_pointee\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         current\cf5 .\cf14 free\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         current \cf5 =\cf14  next_node\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The "head" node stays allocated unless you explicitly free it yourself:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 list_head\cf5 [].\cf14 free_chain\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 list_head\cf5 .\cf14 destroy_pointee\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 list_head\cf5 .\cf14 free\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Destructors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When you build real Mojo data structures, you usually want a safe API that hides raw pointers from users. In a complete linked-list type (rather than a small demo of linkable nodes) the parent list handles node allocation and freeing. Because it owns the nodes, it also performs cleanup in its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 destructor}}.\
Here's a small example based on the Mojo Standard Library that shows how to deinitialize 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 LinkedList\cf5 [\cf14 T\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  _head\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 .\cf14 _NodePointer\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf19 """Clean up the list by freeing all nodes.\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \cb13         Notes:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf19 \cb13             Time complexity: O(n) in len(self).\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf19 \cb13         See Also:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf19 \cb13             "Choose the form of the Destructor!"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf19 \cb13             -- Gozer, "Ghostbusters" (1984).\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf19 \cb13         """\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13         \cf5 var\cf14  curr \cf5 =\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 _head\cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 while\cf14  curr\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 var\cf14  \cf15 next\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  curr\cf5 [].\cf15 next\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             curr\cf5 .\cf14 destroy_pointee\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             curr\cf5 .\cf14 free\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             curr \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 next\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

